  "why": "The statement asks for two things, g(k) and G(k), so even a correct proof of the g(k) formula for all k is only a case (F3). Root's preprint is the only post-snapshot event that touches the question as stated. I marked it not_credible rather than merely doubtful because several F10 signals are present together: it claims a landmark result; it has had no uptake in more than a year; references updated after it still call the formula conjectural and do not cite it; the author has no affiliation; it uses an elementary route on a question that has resisted deep Diophantine-approximation methods; and the formula as extracted has the wrong sign. Li's preprint is dated 21 Jan 2024, five days before the snapshot, and no revision is shown to carry new mathematics, so it stays pre-snapshot. The other post-snapshot papers are variants (positive powers, square plus powers, real exponents, finite fields). Single exact values such as g(k) or G(k) for one k are cases. Kubina–Wunderlich's check up to 471,600,000 is instances. The 1936 reduction is none. Mahler's finitely-many-exceptions result and Beukers's effective large-k result are cases that leave the problem open.",
